  "INTRODUCTION\nThe rich tapestry of South Indian history is vividly coloured \nby the Cholas, Hoysalas, and Pandyas, each dynasty carving \nits unique imprint. The Cholas, from around 850 to 1200 \nCE, stand out for their advanced governance and cultural \nsplendour, marking a significant shift from the Pallava \nera and extending their influence beyond the Indian \nsubcontinent. The Hoysalas, flourishing between the 10th \nand 14th centuries, are renowned for their architectural \nmarvels and cultural contributions in present-day Karnataka. \nThe Pandyas, ruling the southernmost parts of the peninsula, \nwere noted for their pearls and distinctive societal elements, \nincluding potential matriarchal influences.\nThe Cholas: Founders of a Maritime Empire\nFig. 2.1: Medieval South India\nThe Cholas founded a mighty empire that reshaped the \ndynamics of power in southern India from the 9th to the \n12th century CE. Often referred to as the ‘Imperial Cholas \nof Tanjore,’ their lineage, though unclearly linked to the \nearlier Cholas mentioned in the Sangam literature marked \na period of unparalleled expansion and dominance. The \nCholas’ rise to power involved the strategic overthrow of the \nPallavas in South India, and their domain soon encompassed \na significant part of the Indian peninsula.\nThe Chola dynasty is particularly noted for its naval \ncapabilities, a cornerstone of its military and economic \nstrength. Their formidable navy not only secured the empire’s \ncoastal borders but also played a key role in expanding \nits maritime trade across the Indian Ocean. This naval \nstrength enabled them to conquer strategically important \nregions like Sri Lanka and the Maldives and to assert their \ndominance over South-east Asian territories such as Malaya, \nJava, and Sumatra. These victories and expansions not only \nunderscored their military prowess but also opened up new \navenues for trade and cultural exchanges.\nVijayalaya, the 9th-century founder of the Chola dynasty, \nwas instrumental in establishing the foundation for what \nwould become one of the most significant empires in South \nIndian history. His ascendancy from a Pallava vassal to a \nsovereign ruler marked the beginning of a new era of Chola \ndominance that would have lasting implications for the \nregion’s political and cultural landscape. The Cholas’ system \nof governance, particularly their emphasis on local self￾administration, set them apart from their contemporaries \nand predecessors, showcasing a model of decentralised \ngovernance that was ahead of its time.\nIGNITE YOUR MIND\nDo you think the Chola rise to power primarily \nrevolved around their exceptional naval capabilities, \nor were there other key factors, such as strategic \nalliances, administrative reforms, and leadership, \nthat played equally crucial roles in their expansion \nand dominance?\nIMPORTANT CHOLA RULERS\nVijayalaya Chola: The Founder of the Chola \nDynasty (c.850-870 CE)\n Rise to Power: Vijayalaya, the architect of the Chola \nempire, began his ascent to power around 850 CE. Initially \na feudatory under the Pallavas, Vijayalaya capitalised on \nthe declining influence of his overlords to establish his \nown realm. \n• He strategically extended his power in the region \naround Eraiyur, capturing the vital city of Tanjore. \n• This marked the beginning of Chola’s territorial \nexpansion, with Vijayalaya extending his kingdom \nalong the fertile lower Kaveri region.\n Establishing Tanjore as Capital: Vijayalaya’s choice of \nTanjore as the capital was pivotal. It served not just as a \npolitical centre but also as a cultural and religious hub for \nthe Cholas. Under his rule, Tanjore began to evolve into a \ncity of significant importance, laying the groundwork for \nthe cultural renaissance that the dynasty would later be \nknown for.\nSouthern India \n(c. 850 - 1200 CE) 2"
