  "Southern India (c. 850 - 1200 CE) 15\nArumolivarman/Rajaraja I: The Pinnacle of \nChola Glory (c. 985−1014 CE)\n Unprecedented Expansion and Military Achievements:\nArumolivarman, better known as Rajaraja I, is often \nhailed as the greatest ruler of the Chola dynasty. His \nreign from 985 to 1014 CE marked the zenith of Chola \npower, with the empire expanding to become the largest \ndominion in South India until the 13th century. Rajaraja \nI’s military campaigns were characterised by strategic \nbrilliance and relentless ambition.\n Breaking Confederations and Expanding Territories: \nRajaraja I conducted a series of successful military \ncampaigns that shattered the confederations of regional \npowers like the Pandyas, the rulers of Kerala, and Sri \nLanka. His decisive actions against the Chera navy at \nThiruvananthapuram and his attacks on Kollam (Quilon)\nwere instrumental in breaking the maritime power of the \nCheras. Furthermore, his conquest of Madurai expanded \nthe Chola empire into Pandya territory.\n Annexation of Northern Sri Lanka and the Maldives: \nOne of Rajaraja I’s significant accomplishments was the \nannexation of Northern Sri Lanka, which he renamed \nMummadichola Mandalam. This move not only \ndemonstrated the Chola’s naval supremacy but also \ntheir ability to govern diverse territories. Additionally, he \nextended Chola’s influence to the Maldives, consolidating \nthe empire’s control over crucial maritime routes.\n Naval Expeditions and Overseas Influence: Rajaraja I’s \nnaval prowess was further displayed in his expedition \nagainst the Sailendra Empire in the Malay Peninsula, \nwhich opened new avenues for trade and cultural \nexchange with China. This expansion was not just a \ndisplay of military might but also a strategic step in \nestablishing the Cholas as a dominant maritime power \nin the region.\n Conflicts with Western Chalukyas and Later Chalukyas:\nHis reign also saw intense conflicts with the Western \nChalukyas and the later Chalukyas, who had their capital \nat Kalyani. Rajaraja I’s campaigns led to the annexation of \nthe northWestern parts of the Ganga region in northwest \nKarnataka and the overrun of Vengi. \n• Despite the conflicts, Rajaraja I showed diplomatic \nacumen by restoring Vengi to its rulers after a \nmatrimonial alliance, marrying the daughter of the \nking of Vengi. \n• The prolonged Chola-Chalukya conflicts over regions \nlike Vengi, the Tungabhadra doab, and the Ganga \ncountry, though exhaustive, were pivotal in shaping \nthe political landscape of South India.\n Architectural and Administrative Legacy: In c. 1010 CE, \nRajaraja I commissioned the construction of the majestic \nRajarajesvara or Brihadeshwara Temple in Thanjavur. \nThis architectural marvel, dedicated to Lord Shiva, is not \nonly a symbol of Chola religious devotion but also of their \npatronage of arts and architecture. The practice of installing \nimages of kings and queens in temples, alongside deities, \nwas prevalent during his reign, reflecting the divine status \nof the monarchy.\n Revenue System and Land Survey: Rajaraja I’s \nadministrative skills were exemplified in his overhaul \nof the revenue system. He initiated a comprehensive \nland survey and assessment, ensuring efficient tax \ncollection and state resource management. His title \n‘Ulagalandha Perumal’ (the great one who measured the \nearth) is reflective of both his imperial ambitions and his \nmeticulous administrative reforms.\nFig. 2.3: Brihadeshwara Temple\nRajendra I: The Architect of a Vast Empire \n(c. 1014−1044 CE)\n Ascendancy and Conquests: Rajendra I, son of the \nillustrious Rajaraja I, ascended the Chola throne around \n1014 CE, inheriting not only an empire but also his \nfather’s expansionist vision. His reign was characterised \nby extensive military campaigns that further expanded \nthe territorial reach of the Chola dynasty.\n Expansions in the South: Rajendra I continued his \nfather’s policy of southern expansion by subjugating \nthe Pandyas and Cheras, bringing their territories under \nChola control. This consolidation further cemented the \nCholas as the preeminent power in South India.\n Triumph Over Sri Lanka: One of Rajendra’s most notable \nmilitary achievements was the definitive conquest of Sri \nLanka. By defeating Sri Lankan King Mahinda V, Rajendra \nI not only captured the royal insignia of the Sri Lankan \nking and queen but also ensured Chola’s dominance \nover the island for the next half-century. This victory was \nmore than a military triumph; it was a statement of the \nChola’s naval and military supremacy in the region.\n Northern Expedition and Gangaikondacholapuram:\nIn 1022 CE, Rajendra I embarked on a bold Northern \nexpedition, echoing the legendary conquests of \nSamudragupta. He crossed the Ganga, defeating the Pala \nruler Mahipal I and the Western Chalukyas. \n• To commemorate his victorious march up to the \nGanges, he assumed the title ‘Gangaikondachola’\n(the Chola who conquered the Ganga) and established \na new capital named Gangaikondacholapuram. \n• Here, he built a magnificent Shiva temple and excavated \na large water reservoir, Chodagarg, symbolising his \narchitectural and administrative prowess."
