  "18 Medieval India \nAdministration and Governance\n Royal Tours: Kings often undertook tours to stay in touch \nwith the administration across their vast empire. These \ntours were also a means to exhibit royal authority and \ninteract with local administrations.\n Royal Bodyguards: Kings were protected by loyal \nbodyguards, chosen through a hereditary system. These \nbodyguards had significant importance and were given \nassignments of land revenue. Interestingly, Marco Polo, \na Venetian traveller, noted that these bodyguards would \ncommit self-immolation upon the monarch’s death.\n Royal Household and the Grandeur: The Chola kings \nmaintained large households and palatial residences, \ncomplete with banquet halls, gardens, and terraces, \nreflecting the grandeur of their reign.\nExpansion of the Administrative Structure\n Growth and Decline of Bureaucracy: From the reign of \nRajaraja I in the early 11th century, there was a notable \nexpansion in the administrative structure. However, \npost the reign of Kulothunga I, there was a decline in \ncentralised bureaucracy and an increase in the power of \nlocal chieftains.\n Military Organisation: The Cholas maintained a large \nand diverse army, comprising elephants, cavalry, and \ninfantry armed with spears.\nTerritorial and Local Administration\n Provinces and Governance: The empire, known \nas ‘Rajyam’ or ‘Rastryam’, was divided into eight \n‘mandalams’ or provinces, each headed by a viceroy \nor governor. These governors were usually princes or \nmembers of noble families.\n Local Administration: The provinces were further divided \ninto ‘Kottams’ or ‘Valanadus’, and then into ‘Nadus’ \n(districts). Each Nadu consisted of several autonomous \nvillages, which were crucial to the administrative system.\n Nattar and Nagarattar Assemblies: The ‘Nattar’ was \nan assembly of leading men in a district, while the \n‘Nagarattar’ or ‘Nagaram’ was an assembly of mercantile \ngroups or merchants, specific to different trades.\nRole of Guilds and Merchant Associations\n Shrenis or Pugas: The Cholas’ administration was \nfacilitated by guilds or ‘shrenis’, autonomous corporate \norganisations comprising people from the same craft.\n Diverse and Specialized Guilds: These included guilds like \nSaliya nagaram (textile trade), Shankarappadi nagaram\n(oil and ghee suppliers), Paraga nagaram (seafaring \nmerchants), and Vaniya nagaram (oil merchants). These \nguilds operated under a contract or agreement (‘samaya’) \nand followed a code of conduct called ‘bananju-dharma’.\n Prominent Guilds: ‘Ayyavole’ and ‘Manigramam’ were \namong the most powerful and largest guilds, indicating \na sophisticated network of trade and commerce. These \nguilds had enough influence to fix cesses and tolls and \noften moved with armed protection.\nThe Chola administration, a hallmark of medieval South \nIndian governance, demonstrated a sophisticated balance of \ncentralised authority and local autonomy, backed by a robust \nmilitary and supported by an intricate network of trade and \ncommerce through guilds and merchant associations. This \nsystem not only facilitated effective control over a vast \nempire but also allowed for significant local participation \nand economic development.\nCHOLA VILLAGE ADMINISTRATION: \nA PRECURSOR TO PANCHAYATI \nRAJ\nThe Chola Empire’s village administration system stands as \nan early and sophisticated example of local self-governance, \nakin to the modern Panchayati Raj system in India. This system \nwas highly systematic and well-developed, as evidenced by \nvarious Chola inscriptions.\nTypes of Village Assemblies\n Ur (General Assembly): The ‘Ur’ was an assembly of \nlocal residents in non-brahmadeya (or vellanvagai)\nvillages, typically comprising fewer than ten members. It \nhandled matters related to land maintenance, including \nsales, gifts, and tax exemptions. The exact composition \nof the ‘Ur’ is not fully known, but it functioned without \nformal rules or procedures.\n Sabha or Mahasabha (Exclusive Assembly of \nBrahmans): This assembly was exclusive to agraharas\n(rent-free brahmadeya villages) and consisted of adult \nmale Brahman members. Membership criteria included \nproperty ownership, family background, learning, and \ngood conduct. The Mahasabha held proprietary rights \nover communal lands and managed private lands within \nits jurisdiction, with the authority to levy taxes and raise \nloans.\n Links to the Chola Court: Some Brahmana Sabhas had \nclose links with the Chola court. Inscriptions from places \nlike Uttaramerur and Tanjavur reveal that resolutions of \nthe Sabha were often made in the presence of officials \ndeputed by the king, illustrating a degree of oversight or \ninteraction with the central authority.\nManagement and Committees\n Executive Committees: Villages were managed by \nexecutive committees composed of educated property \nowners. Members were elected through drawing lots \nor rotation, serving for three years. These committees \noversaw various aspects of village life, including law and \norder, justice, and water distribution (managed by the \n‘erivariya’ or tank committee)."
